Z||000001||Monkeyman||06-09-2001||04:05 PM||||Just a thought, but you might invest in some photo editing software.  That way you can snap some pics of your current interior, then change the color as much as you want.  That way you'll have an idea what it'll look like before you do the work.  Try before you buy.<P>MM||||reg||1
Z||000002||Seanh||06-09-2001||04:13 PM||||Yeah, Iv got PhotoShop, but I'm really not good at doing photoediting at all. Plus, I don't have a scanner either, tons of car picts, no scanner. Plus on top of that, I have a laptop, so photoediting probally wouldn't be too much fun on a mousepad. Oh well, I'll give it a shot and see what happens. Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000003||fyerstarter||06-09-2001||04:37 PM||||The easiest way to colorize stuff in Photoshop is desaturate your original pic so that it is greyscale. Create a new layer and set the type to "color", now either paint in that layer with what color you want to colorize the underlying layer, or else make a selection and fill it. You can add multiple layers and set the transparency to vary how saturated the color is. It's real easy, if you have a hard time finding these things I mentioned hit F1  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
